    {
      "step_id": "tile_sql_fund_relational_select.s05",
      "type": "explainer",
      "concept_ids": [
        "sql.select_fundamentals"
      ],
      "eyebrow": "SELECT Basics",
      "title": "SELECT and FROM: pulling columns out of a table",
      "lede": "A SELECT statement names the columns you want and the table to read them from.",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "prose",
          "block_id": "tile_sql_fund_relational_select.s05.b01",
          "role": "intro",
          "text": "The customers table has columns customer_id, name and city. To pull that data out of the database you write a SELECT statement. The SELECT list — everything between SELECT and FROM — specifies the expressions that form the output rows, and the FROM clause names the table the query reads from.",
          "lineage": {
            "concept_ids": [
              "sql.select_fundamentals"
            ],
            "teaching_block_ids": [
              "sql.select_fundamentals#tb1",
              "sql.select_fundamentals#tb2"
            ],
            "fact_ids": [
              "sql.select_fundamentals#f1",
              "sql.select_fundamentals#f3"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "prose",
          "block_id": "tile_sql_fund_relational_select.s05.b02",
          "role": "syntax",
          "text": "You can rename an output column by writing AS output_name after its expression in the SELECT list — AS can be left out if the name you want does not collide with a keyword. SELECT name AS customer_name, city FROM customers returns the same data, but the first column is labelled customer_name in the result.",
          "lineage": {
            "concept_ids": [
              "sql.select_fundamentals"
            ],
            "teaching_block_ids": [
              "sql.select_fundamentals#tb3"
            ],
            "fact_ids": [
              "sql.select_fundamentals#f2"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code_walkthrough",
          "block_id": "tile_sql_fund_relational_select.s05.b03",
          "language": "sql",
          "code": "SELECT name AS customer_name,\n       city\nFROM   customers;",
          "annotations": [
            {
              "start_line": 1,
              "end_line": 2,
              "text": "SELECT list: the expressions that form the output rows — name is renamed to customer_name, city keeps its name."
            },
            {
              "start_line": 3,
              "end_line": 3,
              "text": "FROM clause: derives the table the query reads from — here the customers table."
            }
          ],
          "reveal": "line_by_line",
          "lineage": {
            "concept_ids": [
              "sql.select_fundamentals"
            ],
            "teaching_block_ids": [
